00:05:33.400 --> 00:05:40.400
So let's have a look at the
Non-Manufacturing Index of Services PMI.

00:05:40.400 --> 00:05:48.400
And a preliminary report, so preliminary
report, is published in the first week of
each month.

00:05:48.400 --> 00:05:52.400
And there's a final report that's
published at the end of the month.

00:05:52.400 --> 00:05:54.400
The link is on the screen there.

00:05:54.400 --> 00:06:04.400
You can go to ismworld.org and search for
the ISM Non-Manufacturing Report on
Business.

00:06:04.400 --> 00:06:07.400
And the NMI is important.

00:06:07.400 --> 00:06:08.400
Why?

00:06:08.400 --> 00:06:14.400
Because the service sector represents 80%
of the U.S. economy.

00:06:14.400 --> 00:06:19.400
But there is a slight data drawback with
it.

00:06:19.400 --> 00:06:29.400
The composite data, so the aggregate data
for the headline index, only goes back to
January 2008.

00:06:29.400 --> 00:06:36.400
So when we're analyzing the aggregate
composite index and we're assessing
business cycles,

00:06:36.400 --> 00:06:43.400
there's obviously a lot less business
cycles that we can look at versus the ISM
Manufacturing Report.

00:06:43.400 --> 00:06:56.400
So purely from a historical data
perspective, the NMI naturally is less
statistically significant than the ISM
Manufacturing Index.

00:06:56.400 --> 00:07:01.400
Now, some would argue that makes the ISM
Manufacturing Index more important.

00:07:01.400 --> 00:07:11.400
From a traders and PM's perspective, it's
best to basically put an equal weighting
on these.

00:07:11.400 --> 00:07:22.400
Because the NMI covers the service sector,
and that's 80% of the U.S. economy, it's
very, very important.

00:07:22.400 --> 00:07:32.400
It obviously is very important, even
though at the aggregate level, we only
have data going back to 2008.

00:07:32.400 --> 00:07:45.400
You'll see later in the video that
actually the components data of the NMI
actually goes back to 1997.

00:07:45.400 --> 00:08:02.400
So from a traders and PM's perspective, we
basically, in level of importance, don't
discriminate against the NMI just because
it has composite data only going back to
Jan 2008.

00:08:02.400 --> 00:08:11.400
We treat it as equal importance, if not
more importance than the ISM Manufacturing
Index.

00:08:11.400 --> 00:08:15.400
Some in the market would argue the other
way around.

00:08:15.400 --> 00:08:27.400
But to be prudent from a traders and
portfolio managers perspective, we just
put an equal weighting on both indices in
terms of level of importance.

00:08:27.400 --> 00:08:33.400
The other thing that we have to consider
is that it is in the service industry.

00:08:33.400 --> 00:08:36.400
So this includes contracts.

00:08:36.400 --> 00:08:44.400
It's non-physical or not real goods bought
and sold, unlike the Manufacturing Index.

00:08:44.400 --> 00:08:55.400
Also, in terms of composition, there's
small differences in the makeup of the NMI
versus the ISM Manufacturing Report.

00:08:55.400 --> 00:09:13.400
But again, as traders and portfolio
managers, because the service sector
represents such a big part of the US
economy now, we have to treat them in
exactly the same way when using them as a
proxy for portfolio direction.

00:09:13.400 --> 00:09:24.400
So when we're trying to ascertain whether
we should have a long, short or neutral
bias in our portfolios.

00:09:24.400 --> 00:09:35.400
Now, the NMI has four areas that are
covered to make up the Composite Index.

00:09:35.400 --> 00:09:47.400
So there are four components with an equal
weighting of 25% that make up the
aggregate ISM non-manufacturing Index.

00:09:47.400 --> 00:10:05.400
So we have business activity, new orders,
employment and deliveries, all with an
equal 25% weighting that make up the
overall PMI for the non-manufacturing
Index.

00:10:05.400 --> 00:10:13.400
By far the most important are business
activity and new orders, which we're going
to come on to a little bit later.

00:10:13.400 --> 00:10:26.400
But the survey itself surveys 370
purchasing and supply executives in 18
service industry sectors.

00:10:26.400 --> 00:10:41.400
Now, in terms of design of the survey,
very similar to the ISM Manufacturing
Index, we are calculating a Diffusion
Index.

00:10:41.400 --> 00:10:53.400
So the Institute for Supply Management
calculates the Service Sector PMI as a
Diffusion Index in the same way as the
Manufacturing Index.

00:10:53.400 --> 00:11:04.400
So they do this for the four subsurveys or
indicators that have the equal weighting
and then calculate the composite number.

00:11:04.400 --> 00:11:14.400
So the respondents are basically
indicating in the same way that the
Manufacturing Index respondents do whether
particular activities.

00:11:14.400 --> 00:11:28.400
So for example, business activity or even
new orders for their organizations have
either increased, decreased or remained
unchanged from the previous month.

00:11:28.400 --> 00:11:39.400
Now, if you need a recap on how a
Diffusion Index is calculated, you can
obviously go back to the previous video
where, on the slides that you see on the
screen,

00:11:39.400 --> 00:11:46.400
you get a full breakdown of how a
Diffusion Index is calculated.

00:11:46.400 --> 00:11:54.400
And the Non-Manufacturing Report on
Business, the Services PMI is exactly the
same as the Manufacturing PMI,

00:11:54.400 --> 00:12:01.400
in that it's a Diffusion Index of the
subcategories and then the composite
number is calculated.

00:12:01.400 --> 00:12:16.400
Now, in terms of how we read it, it's
again pretty much the same as the
Manufacturing Index, just different
subcomponents.

00:12:16.400 --> 00:12:30.400
So in terms of our expansion and
contraction, the 50 level is our important
level in the Diffusion Index and the
Composite Index as well.

00:12:30.400 --> 00:12:39.400
So we read the Non-Manufacturing Composite
PMI in exactly the same way in which we
read the ISM Manufacturing PMI.

00:12:39.400 --> 00:12:51.400
Now, a reading in the composite number
above 50 indicates general growth, so
expansion, in the services sector versus
the previous month.

00:12:51.400 --> 00:13:03.400
A reading in the composite number below 50
indicates contraction, so general
contraction in the services sector versus
the previous month.

00:13:03.400 --> 00:13:10.400
And a reading of 50 itself indicates that
there's no change in conditions from the
previous month.

00:13:10.400 --> 00:13:31.400
Now, in terms of its usefulness, as a
leading indicator, it's great for traders
and PMs to know what's going on in the US
service sector in terms of expected future
expansion and contraction.

00:13:31.400 --> 00:13:34.400
And we can glean a lot of information from
it.

00:13:34.400 --> 00:13:41.400
So it has a lot of implications for
traders similar to the ISM Manufacturing.

00:13:41.400 --> 00:13:47.400
So using it to predict US GDP growth and
global GDP growth.

00:13:47.400 --> 00:13:57.400
So a general expectation of overall
expansion and contraction in both the US
and global economy.

00:13:57.400 --> 00:14:03.400
We can use it to predict overall stock
market direction and potential returns.

00:14:03.400 --> 00:14:07.400
So we want to know which way the wind is
blowing.

00:14:07.400 --> 00:14:19.400
Therefore, predicting US stock market
direction and potential percentage returns
of the stock market.

00:14:19.400 --> 00:14:28.400
It helps us, of course, understand and
potentially predict likely Federal Reserve
policy.

00:14:28.400 --> 00:14:29.400
Why?

00:14:29.400 --> 00:14:32.400
Because the Fed is looking at this number
as well.

00:14:34.400 --> 00:14:47.400
We also get an appreciation and
understanding due to our general awareness
of overall expansion or contraction and
which way the wind is blowing.

00:14:47.400 --> 00:15:02.400
We also get a bias, a potential bias in
our portfolios, whether we should be
running a net long, a net short or neutral
portfolio.

00:15:02.400 --> 00:15:12.400
Additionally, we get quantitative and
qualitative information of the industries
that make up the services sector.

00:15:12.400 --> 00:15:24.400
So we can use that information to look at
potential individual single stock trade
ideas for our portfolios.

00:15:24.400 --> 00:15:42.400
Now, we never rely on simply one indicator
or even a few indicators to get our
potential long, short or neutral bias in
our portfolios when we're predicting
macroeconomic conditions in the future.

00:15:42.400 --> 00:15:46.400
So US GDP and stock market direction.

00:15:46.400 --> 00:15:58.400
And we also don't use one or just a few
leading indicators to try and get single
stock trade ideas for our portfolios.

00:15:58.400 --> 00:16:01.400
We use them all in conjunction with each
other.

00:16:01.400 --> 00:16:28.400
So the non-manufacturing report on
business can be used obviously and should
be used in conjunction with our money
market leading indicators that we covered
in videos four to six and the ISM
manufacturing report on business and other
macroeconomic indicators that we're going
to be covering in the rest of the PTM
video series.

00:16:28.400 --> 00:16:40.400
We use them all in conjunction together to
get a very good understanding of the US
economy, money flows and of course,
potential Fed policy.

00:16:40.400 --> 00:16:56.400
Now, in terms of survey design included in
the diffusion index, we mentioned this
four areas, business activity, new orders,
employment and deliveries.

00:16:56.400 --> 00:17:05.400
We are going to be focusing on by far the
most important areas in this video
business activity and new orders.

00:17:05.400 --> 00:17:17.400
Obviously employment and deliveries are
included, but they're not as important in
terms of leading indicators because they
are lagging within the index itself.

00:17:17.400 --> 00:17:40.400
So the contribution that these four
components make to the overall composite
index, although they are equal at 25%
each, by far the most important
contribution from a traders and portfolio
managers perspective is business activity
and new orders.

00:17:40.400 --> 00:18:04.400
What's not included in the diffusion index
just from an educational perspective, and
if you have the time each month to go
through them, they are worth going through
to understand the nuts and bolts of the
survey itself and understand some of the
responses that are recurring there.

00:18:04.400 --> 00:18:26.400
There can be some useful information to be
gleaned from that, but nowhere near as
important as business activity and new
orders that are actually included in the
diffusion index and make a very important
contribution from a leading indicator and
traders and portfolio managers
perspective.

00:18:26.400 --> 00:18:39.400
So in your own time, you can self-educate
on inventories, prices, order backlog, new
export orders, imports and inventory
sentiment.

00:18:39.400 --> 00:18:43.400
We're not going to be covering it in this
video itself.

00:18:43.400 --> 00:18:50.400
We're going to be focusing on, as I say,
the most important numbers, business
activity and new orders.

00:18:50.400 --> 00:18:59.400
So remember, we're looking at the
contributors here to the composite
non-manufacturing purchasing managers
index PMI.

00:18:59.400 --> 00:19:06.400
And we're looking at the diffusion index
and the most important ones contributing
to that composite.

00:19:06.400 --> 00:19:10.400
So business activity, what does it cover?

00:19:10.400 --> 00:19:23.400
Well, it tells us whether business
activity is increasing or decreasing
overall in the US service sector.

00:19:23.400 --> 00:19:31.400
And then new orders reflects the level of
new orders from customers of service
sector businesses.

00:19:31.400 --> 00:19:35.400
So the US service sector in its entirety.

00:19:35.400 --> 00:19:57.400
So taken together, these two are the most
important ones because we're getting a
general month on month comparison of what
is happening in terms of business activity
and new orders within the US service
sector, which remember is 80% of the US
economy.

00:19:57.400 --> 00:20:09.400
Employment is a lagging component of this
survey and will very likely follow
business activity and new orders.

00:20:09.400 --> 00:20:26.400
And supplier deliveries gives us an idea
of the efficiency of the supply chain and
can give us an indication of the
bullishness or bearishness of
non-manufacturing or services.

00:20:26.400 --> 00:20:36.400
Companies on their supply chain and the
bullishness or bearishness of suppliers
themselves on the supply chain.

00:20:36.400 --> 00:20:39.400
But we have to remember that this is the
service sector.

00:20:39.400 --> 00:20:43.400
This is not physical goods bought and
sold.

00:20:43.400 --> 00:20:55.400
So from a trader's portfolio manager's
perspective, because the supply chain is
not physical or real goods bought and
sold.

00:20:55.400 --> 00:21:03.400
So like widgets manufactured in a factory
and there's a supply chain behind that.

00:21:03.400 --> 00:21:20.400
From a trader's and portfolio manager's
perspective, supplier deliveries is not as
important as supplier deliveries in the
manufacturing sector and in the NMI
itself, nowhere near as important as
business activity and new orders.

00:21:20.400 --> 00:21:32.400
And employment is a lagging component,
which is likely to follow new orders and
business activity.

00:21:32.400 --> 00:21:40.400
So we don't completely obviously ignore
employment and supplier deliveries.

00:21:40.400 --> 00:21:48.400
They are contributing to the composite
number as components in there.

00:21:48.400 --> 00:22:02.400
But the composite number, because it's
affected by employment and supplier
deliveries, we need to dig a little deeper
and look at business activity and new
orders.

00:22:02.400 --> 00:22:12.400
And in terms of importance, but a much
higher weighting on business activity and
new orders from a trader's and portfolio
manager's perspective.

00:22:12.400 --> 00:22:24.400
Now, there are, as I mentioned earlier,
areas in the survey that respondents
provide information on that are not
included in the diffusion index.

00:22:24.400 --> 00:22:36.400
So inventories, prices, backlog of orders,
new export orders, imports, and inventory
sentiment in your own time.

00:22:36.400 --> 00:22:40.400
So in time, you can self-educate on these
areas.

00:22:40.400 --> 00:22:42.400
That's totally fine.

00:22:42.400 --> 00:22:46.400
You've got all the time in the world to go
and do that on your own.

00:22:46.400 --> 00:22:55.400
Backlog of orders, in terms of level of
importance, is probably the area that you
should be focusing on.

00:22:55.400 --> 00:23:07.400
Why? Because backlog of orders gives us a
general understanding of whether the
service sector in the US is growing or
declining.

00:23:07.400 --> 00:23:21.400
If the service sector's backlog of orders
is growing, for example, it's probably
because business activity and new orders
are growing and the backlog is growing in
response to that.

00:23:21.400 --> 00:23:32.400
So it's almost like a confirmation of our
previous business activity and new orders
analysis from previous months.

00:23:32.400 --> 00:23:47.400
Additionally, if backlog of orders is
rising continuously, for example, it's
likely that the service sector will
require more resources to fulfil that
business

00:23:47.400 --> 00:23:52.400
and a better supply chain or a more
efficient supply chain.

00:23:52.400 --> 00:23:59.400
Therefore, there will be investment in
employment and the supply chain.

00:23:59.400 --> 00:24:05.400
So employment and supplier deliveries will
respond to that as well.

00:24:05.400 --> 00:24:15.400
So from a traders and portfolio managers
perspective, in the diffusion index,
business activity and new orders, by far
the most important.

00:24:15.400 --> 00:24:30.400
And in addition, responses from survey
participants, not included in the
diffusion index, backlog of orders as a
confirmation indicator.

00:24:30.400 --> 00:24:42.400
Now we have in the downloads area, you may
or may not have noticed already in the
downloads area beneath the video, you'll
see a few files there.

00:24:42.400 --> 00:24:52.400
