Manufacturing Index and also that data
relative to S&P 500 returns and US GDP.

00:25:10.400 --> 00:25:17.400
You'll also notice some charts that have
been compiled from the data.

00:25:17.400 --> 00:25:28.400
What we're looking at here is the NMI
Composite Index and you can see the data
only goes back till January 2008.

00:25:28.400 --> 00:25:46.400
And you can see very clearly two areas of
the charts where the index was
significantly below 50, which was the
global financial crisis of 2008, 2009.

00:25:46.400 --> 00:25:53.400
And also the COVID economic shutdown in
2020.

00:25:53.400 --> 00:26:10.400
You can also clearly see that for a period
of a decade, the US service sector at the
composite level, the non-manufacturing PMI
was above 50 from 2010 to 2020,

00:26:10.400 --> 00:26:18.400
denoting a full decade expansion in the US
service sector.

00:26:18.400 --> 00:26:27.400
You'll also notice in your spreadsheet
downloads, the comparison to quarter on
quarter annualized GDP growth.

00:26:27.400 --> 00:26:46.400
And we can see that in the data that goes
back to 2008, that we get negative real
GDP growth quarter on quarter annualized
in the 2008-2009 financial crisis and
during the COVID economic lockdown.

00:26:46.400 --> 00:27:05.400
And during the expansionary period, for
the vast majority of that period, we get
GDP growth quarter on quarter annualized
going in the same direction positive as
the non-manufacturing index.

00:27:05.400 --> 00:27:20.400
Only on a few occasions did we get one
blip or contraction when the ISM
non-manufacturing index was positive for
that entire decade.

00:27:20.400 --> 00:27:22.400
These were not recessions.

00:27:22.400 --> 00:27:25.400
These were slowdowns in the US economy.

00:27:25.400 --> 00:27:33.400
And there wasn't two quarters of negative
GDP growth as the definition of a
recession.

00:27:33.400 --> 00:27:40.400
They were just one quarter of contraction
due to a slowdown in the US economy.

00:27:40.400 --> 00:27:57.400
And those slowdowns were temporary, not
least because the Fed is looking at the
same information that the market looks at
and Fed policy responded to the slowdown
to avert a recession.

00:27:57.400 --> 00:28:09.400
But if you look at the chart, you can see
in the vast majority of times,
non-manufacturing index and GDP growth
move in the same direction.

00:28:09.400 --> 00:28:19.400
Even though the data is only going back to
2008, we can definitely denote that they
move in the same direction.

00:28:19.400 --> 00:28:24.400
What we also get is our peak and trough
levels.

00:28:24.400 --> 00:28:43.400
So, going back to the previous slide, if
we see the total data composite
non-manufacturing PMI, very simply, we see
around the 60 level.

00:28:43.400 --> 00:28:48.400
This generally denotes a peak in the
non-manufacturing index.

00:28:48.400 --> 00:29:03.400
And between around the level 40 and 45
from previous times when we've had the NMI
below 50 and we've had recessions in the
US.

00:29:03.400 --> 00:29:08.400
Between 40 and 45 is usually the trough
level.

00:29:08.400 --> 00:29:27.400
And if we just look at levels of GDP
growth, GDP growth tends to correspond in
the same direction and peak in terms of
percentage growth around the same times as
non-manufacturing index in the US peaks.

00:29:27.400 --> 00:29:35.400
So, when the service sector peaks, GDP
growth quarter on quarter annualized seems
to peak as well.

00:29:35.400 --> 00:29:43.400
And also trough at the same time, but
moving in the same direction negative and
also troughing at the same time.

00:29:43.400 --> 00:29:59.400
And there's generally also a correlation
there between when the NMI slows down and
comes off from its peak, so does GDP
growth.

00:29:59.400 --> 00:30:08.400
Also in the downloads area, we get the
annual percentage return data for the S&P
500.

00:30:08.400 --> 00:30:14.400
And we compare this to the service sector
PMI as well.

00:30:14.400 --> 00:30:24.400
And we get a similar situation when we
look at this versus when we look at GDP
against the ISM services sector.

00:30:24.400 --> 00:30:41.400
What we see is S&P 500 annual percentage
returns, not just the direction, but the
actual percentage returns themselves, very
closely linked to service sector
performance.

00:30:41.400 --> 00:31:02.400
If we look, for example, in the positive
above 50 area for the decade between 2010
and 2020, if we look between the level of
55 and 60, so 60 being the historical peak
level of the NMI.

00:31:02.400 --> 00:31:14.400
If we look between 55 and 60, this is
generally associated with a obviously
positive stock market performance.

00:31:14.400 --> 00:31:23.400
But in terms of percentage return, it's
generally associated with between 15 and
20%.

00:31:23.400 --> 00:31:45.400
And certainly when we get the peak level
at the 60 level for the NMI, that's where
we see the positive performance, the
positive percentage performance of the S&P
500 peak around 20%.

00:31:45.400 --> 00:32:03.400
That level generally is associated with
the NMI being around 60 and peaking an
annual stock market performance of the S&P
500 of about 20%.

00:32:03.400 --> 00:32:19.400
If we look lower down in the positive area
in the decade 2010 to 2020, if the
composite number is between 50 and 55,
what does this really denote?

00:32:19.400 --> 00:32:35.400
It denotes that there's a slowdown in the
US economy if we're coming from peak and
it generally is associated with positive
stock market data in terms of year on year
performance.

00:32:35.400 --> 00:32:44.400
But the year on year performance is at a
lower level, somewhere between flat and
10% positive performance.

00:32:44.400 --> 00:32:59.400
So, if we look below the 50 level, and we
look at our periods where the NMI was
below 50, and we look at the GDP year on
year performances there,

00:32:59.400 --> 00:33:11.400
you can see from the 2008-2009 period when
we had the global financial crisis and the
US recession,

00:33:11.400 --> 00:33:18.400
you can see that stock market performance
year on year at that time was around
negative 40%.

00:33:18.400 --> 00:33:30.400
In the 2020 scenario, we were looking at
around negative 8%, 9%, 10%, something
like this,

00:33:30.400 --> 00:33:39.400
in the annual performance of the stock
market when the NMI went below 50 during
the COVID economic shutdown.

00:33:39.400 --> 00:33:52.400
Obviously, both of those periods and in
the decade in between, the Fed was
obviously very active, especially in those
periods.

00:33:52.400 --> 00:34:06.400
But we just denote this as traders and PMs
of what we should be expecting if there's
an economic shock, like the global
financial crisis, like the COVID economic
lockdown,

00:34:06.400 --> 00:34:21.400
what level of stock market performance
would we expect when the non-manufacturing
index and even the manufacturing index are
below 50% and what that's associated with.

00:34:21.400 --> 00:34:30.400
So we're understanding business cycle
history here and understanding what type
of S&P 500 returns,

00:34:30.400 --> 00:34:46.400
US stock market returns are associated
with US PMIs in the manufacturing and
non-manufacturing sector during normal and
abnormal conditions.

00:34:46.400 --> 00:34:47.400
US PMIs in the manufacturing.

00:34:47.400 --> 00:34:59.400
Now, I want to emphasize here that there
is no rulebook which says if the ISM
manufacturing or ISM non-manufacturing,

00:34:59.400 --> 00:35:10.400
so services sector, prints at a certain
level in a month, it means we should
expect a certain percentage exactly stock
market return.

00:35:10.400 --> 00:35:11.400
That's not how it works.

00:35:11.400 --> 00:35:16.400
What's happened in the past may not happen
in the future.

00:35:16.400 --> 00:35:23.400
And also, sometimes in the past, we see
that general rules don't work all the
time.

00:35:23.400 --> 00:35:24.400
Of course not.

00:35:24.400 --> 00:35:40.400
So if we look, for example, here on this
chart at the end of 2013 and 2014 period,
we can see that the NMI was printing a
number of times below 55.

00:35:40.400 --> 00:35:45.400
So between 50 and 55, and we still got
stock market performance.

00:35:45.400 --> 00:35:48.400
That was around the 20% level.

00:35:48.400 --> 00:36:01.400
But if you fast forward a little bit, you
can see that when, for example, the NMI
starts to go down slightly, so has peaked
and goes down.

00:36:01.400 --> 00:36:13.400
So if we're looking at 2015, for example,
and 2016, you can see stock market
performance does actually follow the NMI.

00:36:13.400 --> 00:36:19.400
So, yes, as a guide, we can get direction.

00:36:19.400 --> 00:36:24.400
Of course, that's the most important thing
for traders and PMs.

00:36:24.400 --> 00:36:31.400
We can get a good idea of stock market
direction with a very, very high
probability.

00:36:31.400 --> 00:36:44.400
So expansion and contraction in the US
services sector, in the US manufacturing
sector, is a very good guide to GDP and
stock market direction.

00:36:44.400 --> 00:36:49.400
What we can't do, obviously, is pinpoint
exact percentages.

00:36:49.400 --> 00:36:58.400
So the most important thing is direction,
long, short or neutral bias in a
portfolio.

00:36:58.400 --> 00:37:07.400
Now, when we think of the data series
itself, you'll have this in the downloads
area in the spreadsheets.

00:37:07.400 --> 00:37:19.400
When we think of that data series, we
mentioned this earlier, the NMI composite
data, it's only available since January
2008.

00:37:19.400 --> 00:37:28.400
The components data, so the components of
the survey, it goes back to July 1997.

00:37:28.400 --> 00:37:31.400
And we can do a number of things with this
data.

00:37:31.400 --> 00:37:36.400
We can obviously look at the component
data in isolation.

00:37:36.400 --> 00:37:45.400
So we can look at that as a full history
back to July 1997.

00:37:45.400 --> 00:37:53.400
And we can also, obviously, compare the
component data to the aggregate data of
the composite PMI.

00:37:53.400 --> 00:38:10.400
Obviously, though, when we do that,
because the composite data only goes back
to Jan 2008, we can only make that
comparison of the component data from Jan
2008 to present day.

00:38:10.400 --> 00:38:20.400
So, if we have a look at, for example, the
business activity index, we can go all the
way back to the late 90s.

00:38:20.400 --> 00:38:31.400
Here, we've taken the data from 1998
onwards and we get a bit more of a
business cycle history from the service
sector.

00:38:31.400 --> 00:38:47.400
So you can see, for example, in the early
2000s, if you recall, during the tech
wreck where the financial markets
collapsed in the dot-com bust,

00:38:47.400 --> 00:39:01.400
you can see in 2000 that the ISM services
sector business activity index was peaking
and had a few aggressive blips down.

00:39:01.400 --> 00:39:09.400
And that's when GDP began to collapse and
the stock market began to collapse as
well.

00:39:09.400 --> 00:39:24.400
And you can see it goes below 50 in 2001,
recovers in 2002, and then we start an
expansionary period in 2003.

00:39:24.400 --> 00:39:43.400
But what's interesting is that if you
think of the service sector during the
expansionary period of 2003 to 2008, you
can see that it peaked in 2003 to 2005 and
then started a slow decline.

00:39:43.400 --> 00:39:48.400
But really, in a trend declined during
that entire period.

00:39:48.400 --> 00:40:02.400
And then, as we know, the composite index
went below 50 in the great financial
crisis before the financial crisis
actually happened.

00:40:02.400 --> 00:40:22.400
And business activity nosedived as well at
the end 2007 beginning 2008 and took quite
a while to recover after it nosedived
again later in 2008 after spending some
time back above 50.

00:40:22.400 --> 00:40:29.400
So it's good to look at the component data
in the NMI because it does go back a
little bit further.

00:40:29.400 --> 00:40:46.400
Obviously, when we compare it to the ISM
non-manufacturing composite index, if we
take, for example, business activity, we
can only go back to Jan 2008 to make that
comparison.

00:40:46.400 --> 00:40:51.400
And you can see business activity moves in
lockstep with the composite.

00:40:51.400 --> 00:40:52.400
Why?

00:40:52.400 --> 00:40:55.400
Because it's contributing to the
composite.

00:40:55.400 --> 00:41:04.400
And it's probably, along with new orders,
the most important component out of the
four.

00:41:04.400 --> 00:41:15.400
Now, what you can also do in your
spreadsheets is look at business activity
versus the NMI next to each other in terms
of the depth.

00:41:15.400 --> 00:41:19.400
In terms of the data itself, not just on a
chart.

00:41:19.400 --> 00:41:27.400
And you can make comparisons and look at
periods of stock market history to
understand what was really going on.

00:41:27.400 --> 00:41:32.400
You can also do this for the ISM
manufacturing.

00:41:32.400 --> 00:41:35.400
So you can do this for new orders as well.

00:41:35.400 --> 00:41:49.400
One of the components of the ISM
manufacturing composite index and you can
look at that versus the ISM manufacturing
index itself.

00:41:49.400 --> 00:42:05.400
And what we can do is look at periods in
time in stock market history and have a
look at what was going on then for both
business activity and ISM new orders.

00:42:05.400 --> 00:42:23.400
So what we're looking at here, for
example, if we start on the left hand
side, we're looking at NMI business
activity versus the NMI composite for the
period August 2018 to December 2020.

00:42:23.400 --> 00:42:32.400
And if you recall, when we were looking at
manufacturing, when we were looking at ISM
manufacturing data, August 18 was the peak
period.

00:42:32.400 --> 00:42:45.400
And you can see when we look at the NMI
business activity, which is in the first
column, you can see it didn't peak at
August 18.

00:42:45.400 --> 00:42:48.400
It peaked at September 18.

00:42:48.400 --> 00:42:53.400
On the right hand side, you can see the
NMI itself.

00:42:53.400 --> 00:43:00.400
So we've got business activity in the
first column and we've got the NMI in the
second column.

00:43:00.400 --> 00:43:09.400
And in August 18, we have a reading of
58.8 and September 18, a peak of 60.8.

00:43:09.400 --> 00:43:27.400
And then if we look at both business
activity and NMI from August 18 to August
19, which is the period we looked at
previously when we looked at manufacturing
data, you can see a general decline in
both.

00:43:27.400 --> 00:43:34.400
But what's interesting is that business
activity in August 19 was 61 and a half.

00:43:34.400 --> 00:43:40.400
And the NMI composite was in the mid 50s.

00:43:40.400 --> 00:43:43.400
So in August 19, it was 56.

00:43:43.400 --> 00:43:58.400
So if we think about this and we think
back to the ISM manufacturing data,
business activity and the composite is
generally trending lower.

00:43:58.400 --> 00:44:03.400
And the business activity number had
peaked in September 18.

00:44:03.400 --> 00:44:18.400
But when we think about this in terms of
the ISM, we can look at it on the right
hand side and we can see that the ISM new
orders number was 47.2 and the ISM
composite was 48.8.

00:44:18.400 --> 00:44:21.400
So what can we deduce from this?

00:44:21.400 --> 00:44:29.400
We can deduce that in the slowdown in
2018, it was a manufacturing slowdown.

00:44:29.400 --> 00:44:35.400
It was led by the manufacturing sector,
not the service sector.

00:44:35.400 --> 00:44:39.400
The service sector was still in pretty
good shape.

00:44:39.400 --> 00:44:49.400
And if you recall, that was the period
when the Fed was cutting breaks in the
summer of 2019.

00:44:49.400 --> 00:44:54.400
So we have the July cut, the September and
the October cut.

00:44:54.400 --> 00:44:59.400
So what was the Fed responding to at that
time?

00:44:59.400 --> 00:45:10.400
Well, we know from the money markets, our
leading indicators from the money markets,
from both government bond and corporate
bond markets, there was stress there.

00:45:10.400 --> 00:45:20.400
But also, the Fed was responding to a
manufacturing slowdown in the US.

00:45:20.400 --> 00:45:45.400
If we fast forward a little bit and we go
a little bit further in history and we go
to the March, April, May, June 2020
period, if we look at business activity
and the composite NMI, we obviously saw a
big move down because of COVID and the
economic lockdown.

00:45:45.400 --> 00:45:53.400
But what's interesting is that business
activity went below 50 for the first time
in March, 2020.

00:45:53.400 --> 00:45:58.400
And remember, these are reported in the
following month.

00:45:58.400 --> 00:46:03.400
So that number will have been reported in
the first week of April.

00:46:03.400 --> 00:46:07.400
And the NMI itself wasn't below 50.

00:46:07.400 --> 00:46:13.400
So it was a 53.6 reported in April for
March, 2020.

00:46:13.400 --> 00:46:19.400
So what's interesting there is that
business activity is leading the NMI
lower.

00:46:19.400 --> 00:46:32.400
And if we go a little bit further and we
go and we look at the April, May, June
period, we see a trough in business
activity at 26.

00:46:32.400 --> 00:46:39.400
And we see a massive and we see a massive
rebatt for the May number.

00:46:39.400 --> 00:46:42.400
So a massive down move and a massive
rebatt.

00:46:42.400 --> 00:46:59.400
If we look at the ISM new orders versus
ISM manufacturing on the right, if we look
at the April, May and June period, yes, we
get new orders going below 50 in February
2020.

00:46:59.400 --> 00:47:14.400
But when we get the move back up after the
trough, the May 2020 move higher is
nowhere near as big as the magnitude as
the move in business activity for the NMI.

00:47:14.400 --> 00:47:38.400
So when we look at ISM manufacturing
composite number, you can see the recovery
post the Fed rate cuts where the composite
index went back above 50 in January and
February 2020, putting in two readings
above 50.

00:47:38.400 --> 00:47:47.400
And then the new orders index, you can see
that it only puts one reading in above 50
at 52.

00:47:47.400 --> 00:47:49.400
That was in January 2020.

00:47:49.400 --> 00:47:58.400
And then it went below, new orders went
below 50 again in February 2020.

00:47:58.400 --> 00:48:02.400
And then obviously had a big move down in
March 2020.

00:48:02.400 --> 00:48:16.400
But when we compare this, you can see that
going into the COVID collapse and the
COVID economic lockdown, that new orders
was the first to go.

00:48:16.400 --> 00:48:21.400
New orders in the ISM was the first to go
in February 2020.

00:48:21.400 --> 00:48:27.400
So we got a warning there, we got a very
strong warning of a manufacturing
slowdown.

00:48:27.400 --> 00:48:46.400
And as mentioned, the service sector,
there wasn't really a big slowdown
happening, not occurring like in the
manufacturing sector, even when the
period, even during the period when the
Fed was cutting rates, it was still
reported in the mid 50s.

00:48:46.400 --> 00:48:56.400
But then it went below 50 for the March
period, business activity went below 50
for the March period.

00:48:56.400 --> 00:49:04.400
If you look at the composite NMI, it
didn't even go below 50 until the April
reading.

00:49:04.400 --> 00:49:05.400
Okay.

00:49:05.400 --> 00:49:18.400
What's interesting coming out of the sell
off or the slowdown, we know that the
bottom in the S&P 500 was in March.

00:49:18.400 --> 00:49:32.400
But what's interesting is when we look at
business activity and the big, after the
big move down from 48 to 26 for March and
April readings, what's interesting is the
big move up in May to 41.

00:49:32.400 --> 00:49:37.400
And the big move up in June to 66.

00:49:37.400 --> 00:49:57.400
Because when we compare this to new orders
and ISM manufacturing composite, so the
ISM new orders and the ISM manufacturing
composite, when we compare business
activity and the big move down and then
the coming out of it, the two big moves
up.

00:49:57.400 --> 00:50:05.400
What's apparent is that the service sector
rebounded much quicker than the
manufacturing sector.

00:50:05.400 --> 00:50:10.400
So looking at these historical moments,
it's just good to understand what was
going on there.

00:50:10.400 --> 00:50:28.400
The NMI business activity, so business
activity in the service sector is
sometimes a leading indicator to the NMI
itself and even to the ISM, but not all
the time.

00:50:28.400 --> 00:50:35.400
So you have to use these surveys and the
rest of the leading indicators.

00:50:35.400 --> 00:50:50.400
So our money market indicators, so our
money market indicators, you have to use
them all in conjunction and also the
macroeconomic leading indicators that are
coming up in the PTM video series.

00:50:50.400 --> 00:50:54.400
You have to use all of these in
conjunction to understand stock market
history.

00:50:54.400 --> 00:51:02.400
And when you're sitting there at any one
moment in time, you don't know exactly
what's going to happen.

00:51:02.400 --> 00:51:07.400
You need to have all of the information to
make an informed decision.

00:51:07.400 --> 00:51:14.400
If we look at our other important
component of the NMI, that's new orders.

00:51:14.400 --> 00:51:19.400
So we can compare that visually on a
chart.

00:51:19.400 --> 00:51:25.400
We can look at new orders versus the ISM
non-manufacturing index.

00:51:25.400 --> 00:51:29.400
And you can see there's a very, very
strong relationship between the two.

00:51:30.400 --> 00:51:39.400
If we look at our table again over that
period, we can look at May 2020.

00:51:39.400 --> 00:51:44.400
So we're looking at that period again,
August 18 to December 2020.

00:51:44.400 --> 00:51:51.400
And we're looking at NMI business activity
on the left and the composite NMI on the
right.

00:51:51.400 --> 00:52:02.400
And we're looking on the right-hand side
of the screen here at a screenshot of the
May 2020 non-manufacturing survey release.

00:52:03.400 --> 00:52:10.400
And what we're doing is we are comparing
it to ISM manufacturing as well.

00:52:10.400 --> 00:52:18.400
So we're comparing it to the readings
there of the composite level and of
components.

00:52:18.400 --> 00:52:36.400
So what we've highlighted here is the May
2020 survey for both the composite NMI and
business activity or production as it's
called in the survey.

00:52:36.400 --> 00:52:40.400
So we have the NMI composite and business
activity.

00:52:40.400 --> 00:52:43.400
May 2020, the big rebound.

00:52:43.400 --> 00:52:49.400
So you can see the series in April for
business activity was 26.

00:52:49.400 --> 00:52:52.400
And in May 2020, it was 41.

00:52:52.400 --> 00:52:55.400
So this is the big rebound number.

00:52:55.400 --> 00:53:04.400
And we know from hindsight, looking at
history, that there was a move also from
May to June of 41 to 66.

00:53:04.400 --> 00:53:08.400
But we're looking at the May 2020
situation here.

00:53:08.400 --> 00:53:14.400
So imagine you are sitting at this moment
in time and you see the May release.

00:53:14.400 --> 00:53:17.400
So this is released in the first week of
June.

00:53:17.400 --> 00:53:21.400
And you see the May release for
non-manufacturing.

00:53:21.400 --> 00:53:31.400
And you see a massive rebound from 26 to
41 in business activity for the NMI.

00:53:31.400 --> 00:53:41.400
And at the time, imagine yourself
comparing it to the ISM manufacturing
data, which was released just before it.

00:53:41.400 --> 00:53:47.400
So if you look at the manufacturing data,
we look at production for manufacturing.

00:53:47.400 --> 00:53:53.400
The move is from 27.5 to 33.2.

00:53:53.400 --> 00:54:00.400
So a change of 5.7 points versus services,
a change of 15 points.

00:54:00.400 --> 00:54:18.400
So you'd be sitting here at this moment in
time, looking at this survey and realizing
that both the ISM and services have
probably troughed, but services have
rebounded really, really hard.

00:54:18.400 --> 00:54:36.400
And you can see as well from the language
being used to denote the speed of the
contraction reported from the NMI
composite and business activity, that the
direction is still contracting, but at a
slower pace.

00:54:36.400 --> 00:54:38.400
How would you read into this?

00:54:38.400 --> 00:54:40.400
Well, trough conditions.

00:54:40.400 --> 00:54:43.400
Trough conditions have occurred, most
likely.

00:54:43.400 --> 00:54:48.400
And there's been a very hard rebound in
the service sector.

00:54:48.400 --> 00:54:51.400
Even though it's still contracting, it's
contracting slower.

00:54:51.400 --> 00:54:53.400
So we're not out of the woods yet.

00:54:53.400 --> 00:54:58.400
But you certainly would not have a short
bias in your portfolio.

00:54:58.400 --> 00:55:02.400
You would be long to neutral in the
portfolio.

00:55:02.400 --> 00:55:09.400
Now, with a bit of hindsight, we can
understand what was going on in the stock
markets at that time.

00:55:09.400 --> 00:55:19.400
If you recall, the COVID lows were in
March, and then the market rallied pretty
aggressively.

00:55:19.400 --> 00:55:23.400
And we've highlighted two areas here.

00:55:23.400 --> 00:55:27.400
We've highlighted the rebound in the
markets off the lows.

00:55:27.400 --> 00:55:38.400
So the rebound in the S&P 500 off the lows
up until the end of May, beginning of
June, and then from June to the end of
October.

00:55:38.400 --> 00:55:40.400
Why have we done that?

00:55:40.400 --> 00:55:57.400
Well, because we want to look at what the
stock markets did after we realized, or
when we got the information from the ISM
manufacturing and ISM non-manufacturing,
that there was likely trough conditions.

00:55:58.400 --> 00:56:23.400
So what's interesting here is that even if
during the March, April and May period,
you were not participating or trying to
pick the bottom, you still had plenty of
time to get in to a long portfolio bias
after that period.

00:56:23.400 --> 00:56:29.400
And what we mean by not participating
doesn't necessarily mean you don't have
any position at all.

00:56:29.400 --> 00:56:39.400
What it means is that your portfolio is
neutral, and you're not taking a
directional bet in your portfolio either
way.

00:56:39.400 --> 00:56:48.400
The point here is that you didn't even
have to have a dogmatic view either way
during the March, April, May period.

00:56:48.400 --> 00:57:05.400
Then the ISM manufacturing and ISM
non-manufacturing indexes came out with
new orders and business activity
components showing a trough condition in
the first week of June.

00:57:05.400 --> 00:57:10.400
And the business activity number was a big
move higher.

00:57:10.400 --> 00:57:21.400
So the point here is that you could have
just not been dogmatic during that period
with a directional bias either way.

00:57:21.400 --> 00:57:36.400
So the period March, April, May, and then
when you get the leading indicator signal
from the surveys, that's when you know
that there's trough conditions or very
likely to be trough conditions.

00:57:36.400 --> 00:57:42.400
You know, you can't be 100% certain, but
you can be pretty certain that trough
conditions have appeared.

00:57:42.400 --> 00:57:59.400
And if you think about the range here in
the green box of the S&P 500 from first
week of June to October, you know, you're
looking at about a range of 500 points on
a starting point of 3,000.

00:57:59.400 --> 00:58:17.400
So you're looking at about 15, 16% here at
the index level in a period of three
months where it goes from 3,000 to 3,500
from the first week of June till the first
week of September.

00:58:17.400 --> 00:58:24.400
That's obviously a pretty wild move in the
short space of time for an index.

00:58:24.400 --> 00:58:38.400
So again, the point here is that you
really don't have to be very dogmatic in
your portfolio at economic turning points
or during liquidity events.

00:58:38.400 --> 00:58:52.400
So you can sit back, maintain a non-bias
in your portfolio and wait for the leading
indicator macroeconomic signals.

00:58:52.400 --> 00:59:01.400
Generally speaking, you will get returns
by waiting for the leading indicator
signals.

00:59:01.400 --> 00:59:13.400
So what's good about being a student of
macroeconomics and leading indicators in
order to make decisions in your portfolio,
you don't have to be a hero.

00:59:13.400 --> 00:59:20.400
So we don't want to be in a situation all
the time where we're trying to pick the
bottom in the stock market.

00:59:20.400 --> 00:59:24.400
We're trying to pick the top in the stock
market.

00:59:24.400 --> 00:59:32.400
We just lean towards a slight bias or bias
when we get confirmation in the leading
indicators.

00:59:32.400 --> 00:59:39.400
Because the leading indicators lead GDP
and lead stock market direction and
returns.

00:59:39.400 --> 00:59:41.400
The clue is in the name.

00:59:41.400 --> 00:59:42.400
They are leading.

00:59:42.400 --> 00:59:53.400
So when we see peak conditions and trough
conditions, yes, we get a clue as to the
direction we should be leaning towards.

00:59:53.400 --> 00:59:56.400
And that's the direction we lean to.

00:59:56.400 --> 01:00:01.400
But we don't have to try to pick bottoms
and sell tops.

01:00:01.400 --> 01:00:11.400
This is about on balance rotation and
having the right positions at the right
time, which we're going to come on to
later in the video series.

01:00:11.400 --> 01:00:22.400
Having the right positions at the right
time and leaning towards a directional
bias when the leading indicators are
telling us to do so.

01:00:22.400 --> 01:00:27.400
We don't have to fight it and be a hero.

